## First: your Academia.edu hypothesis is only partly right

> “When a paper is only found on Academia.edu, it is usually because the author is bending/breaking copyright rules.”

**Sometimes. But “usually” is too strong.**

The underlying mess is that there are often **three different objects** people casually call “the paper”:

```text
PREPRINT
author's manuscript before peer review

        ↓

AAM / POSTPRINT
accepted manuscript after peer review
but before publisher typesetting

        ↓

VOR
Version of Record
publisher-designed PDF
```

The author may have radically different rights for each.

Springer Nature, for example, explicitly allows authors to self-archive their **accepted manuscript** on their personal website or institutional repository after the applicable embargo. Cambridge likewise has Green OA provisions for pre-final versions. Oxford University Press explicitly allows certain accepted manuscripts in non-commercial repositories after embargo while restricting uploads to **commercial websites/repositories** unless there is an agreement. ([Springer Nature Support][1])

That creates the slightly absurd situation:

> **An author might legally be able to put the accepted manuscript on alexissanderson.com or Oxford's repository while not being permitted to put exactly that version—or especially the publisher PDF—on Academia.edu.**

Academia is not magically exempt from copyright. Its copyright policy says it responds to infringement notices and can terminate repeat infringers. ([Academia][2])

And we've seen this mechanism operate at scale. In December 2013 Elsevier sent Academia.edu roughly **2,800 takedown requests in a few weeks** concerning copies Elsevier asserted rights over. ResearchGate later faced major publisher litigation over essentially the same problem; Elsevier, ACS and ResearchGate reached a settlement in 2023. ([The Washington Post][3])

So Academia's architecture has historically been approximately:

```text
AUTHOR UPLOADS FILE
       ↓
Academia hosts it
       ↓
copyright holder may object
       ↓
DMCA / other legal process
       ↓
Academia removes it
```

rather than:

```text
Academia has negotiated permission
for every PDF on Academia
```

That's an important distinction.

