# So why is Sanderson's work on Academia but not alexissanderson.com?

This is where it gets interesting.

I think there are **at least six mechanisms**, and copyright is only one.

### 1. Academia solved distribution

An author's personal website has essentially no network effect.

Academia currently claims **65 million hosted papers and 310 million members**, and it explicitly sells itself to researchers on visibility, audience growth and impact tracking. ([Academia][4])

Upload once and the paper becomes attached to:

```text
Alexis Sanderson
    ↓
Śaivism
    ↓
Tantra
    ↓
Kashmir
    ↓
people following Sanderson
    ↓
people following related subjects
    ↓
Google search
```

A PDF sitting in `/uploads/6/2/7/...` on a professor's old website doesn't have that machinery.

### 2. Academia gives scholars vanity/impact signals

Academia explicitly provides:

* views;
* downloads;
* search terms;
* reader information;
* mentions;
* profile visitors;
* analytics.

Some of the deeper analytics are Premium features. ([Academia Support][5])

That sounds trivial until you realize academics otherwise often publish something and have almost no idea who actually reads it.

### 3. Personal academic websites die

This is exactly what we just experienced with Sanderson.

```text
alexissanderson.com
        ↓
PDF uploaded
        ↓
years pass
        ↓
domain/site infrastructure changes
        ↓
old URLs die
```

Meanwhile Academia keeps the profile alive.

That is a **preservation problem**, not merely a copyright problem.

### 4. Authors may not understand their own publication contracts

Scholarly publishing rights are genuinely complicated. Creative Commons' OA educational materials note that traditional publication frequently involves authors transferring copyright or granting exclusive publishing rights. ([Creative Commons][6])

Then policies differ by:

```text
publisher
journal
year
article type
preprint/AAM/VOR
embargo
repository type
licence
funder
country
```

It is unsurprising that academics sometimes just upload the PDF they have.

### 5. Enforcement isn't instantaneous

Copyright systems generally do not imply that every unauthorized file is detected the moment someone uploads it.

The historical Academia and ResearchGate disputes demonstrate that large collections can exist until publishers identify files and initiate takedown processes. ([The Scholarly Kitchen][7])

### 6. Academia gives scholars something publishers don't

Publishers mainly optimize around:

> publication.

Academia optimizes around:

> **the scholar.**

That's why the profile is so powerful.

And this connects directly to your earlier idea of putting **every Tantra scholar's canonical page on Pāṭala**, then pulling together books, papers, manuscripts, translations, YouTube lectures, Google Scholar records, Academia records, institutional pages etc.

