# There are good economic precedents — but nobody has combined them this way

### arXiv

arXiv demonstrates that researchers will adopt a field repository as **infrastructure** rather than merely another publication venue. It is community-supported and institutionally funded; Cornell describes its current annual budget as around $6 million and funding from multiple sources. ([Cornell Tech][10])

### Zenodo

Zenodo demonstrates:

```text
free deposit
+
persistent preservation
+
DOI/citation
+
research reporting integration
```

It is operated through CERN/OpenAIRE infrastructure and explicitly emphasizes giving researchers credit through citable outputs. ([Zenodo][11])

### Humanities Commons

This is particularly relevant because **the humanities are different from physics**. Humanities Commons is a nonprofit professional network combining profiles, discussion and a repository rather than merely hosting preprints. ([Humanities Commons][12])

### PubPub

PubPub demonstrates the possibility of an open-source, community-run scholarly publishing infrastructure covering journals, books, manuscripts, annotations, peer review and other objects. ([PubPub][13])

### Subscribe to Open

Subscribe to Open demonstrates another important principle: **institutions can collectively pay so that everybody gets access**. Existing subscribers continue funding a publication; when sufficient support exists, the content becomes openly accessible. ([The Scholarly Kitchen][14])

### Knowledge Unlatched

Similar collective-funding ideas have also been applied to scholarly books through libraries pooling resources to make titles OA. ([Katina Magazine][15])

None of these by itself is your model.

But together they establish the pieces.

